(777615, 58085, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings...<br/> <br/>Guns and Roses are things that are generally associated for the purpose of showing differences rather than similarities.  For a "Things that go together" challenge, this might not be the strongest choice of items for that reason.  This combination of items have a yin/yang type balance of opposites rather than a harmonious balance in the way you might consider pancakes and maple syrup.  <br/> <br/>Your photograph feels a bit static in its composition.  The soft focus doesn''t seem to work particularly well here due to the nature of your subjects.  The perspective from wich you chose to make this photo doesn''t give the viewer any sense of depth.  A different angle... maybe looking at the rose and the barrel of the gun from slightly above would create a nice depth perspective for this photo and still relay the sense of ''togetherness'' that you were aiming for in this image.  I also generally like dark backgrounds, but in this particular case, your subjects appear to be floating in space with no ''grounding''.  Having some visible surface is not a bad thing in most cases... especially when the subject(s) of the photograph are stationary and inanimate.  <br/> <br/>Good luck in future challenges :)<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/></td>', 'Greetings... Guns and Roses are things that are generally associated for the purpose of showing differences rather than similarities.  For a "Things that go together" challenge, this might not be the strongest choice of items for that reason.  This combination of items have a yin/yang type balance of opposites rather than a harmonious balance in the way you might consider pancakes and maple syrup.   Your photograph feels a bit static in its composition.  The soft focus doesn''t seem to work particularly well here due to the nature of your subjects.  The perspective from wich you chose to make this photo doesn''t give the viewer any sense of depth.  A different angle... maybe looking at the rose and the barrel of the gun from slightly above would create a nice depth perspective for this photo and still relay the sense of ''togetherness'' that you were aiming for in this image.  I also generally like dark backgrounds, but in this particular case, your subjects appear to be floating in space with no ''grounding''.  Having some visible surface is not a bad thing in most cases... especially when the subject(s) of the photograph are stationary and inanimate.   Good luck in future challenges :) John Setzler', '2004-06-24 06:30:18.000000', NULL, 0),
